## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design non-intrusive monitoring in KCNA, track per-partition state, and emit reliable alerts without affecting throughput.

## Key Concepts
- Per-partition lag tracking
- End offsets vs committed offsets
- Non-intrusive monitoring
- Idempotent alerting and deduplication

## Code Example
```javascript
class LagMonitor {
  constructor(threshold, windowMs = 60000) {
    this.threshold = threshold;
    this.windowMs = windowMs;
    this.lagMap = new Map();
    this.lastAlert = new Map();
  }
  onMessage(partition, endOffset, committedOffset) {
    const lag = endOffset - committedOffset;
    const now = Date.now();
    this.lagMap.set(partition, { lag, ts: now });
    this.check(partition);
  }
  check(partition) {
    const entry = this.lagMap.get(partition);
    if (!entry) return;
    if (entry.lag > this.threshold && (Date.now() - entry.ts) >= this.windowMs) {
      this.emitAlert(partition, entry.lag);
      this.lastAlert.set(partition, Date.now());
      // reset timer for next alert window
      this.lagMap.set(partition, { lag: entry.lag, ts: Date.now() });
    }
  }
  emitAlert(partition, lag) {
    // publish to 'lag_alerts' with partition, lag, timestamp, host
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for dynamic partitions or multiple topics?
- How would you test alerting under network jitter and partitions rebalance?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests a beginner KCNA candidate's ability to implement a time-window join across topics, with late data, offset handling, and at-least-once guarantees. It requires simple in-memory state management, per-key routing, and clear API boundaries.

## Key Concepts

- Time-window joins across topics
- Late-arriving data handling
- Per-message offset tracking
- Idempotent emissions

## Code Example

```javascript
// Implementation sketch for in-memory join
class JoinEnricher {
  constructor() {
    this.latestAnnotation = new Map(); // serviceId -> { annotation, ts }
    this.metricBuffer = new Map(); // serviceId -> [{metric, ts}]
  }
  processMetric(metric, offset, ts) {
    const ann = this.latestAnnotation.get(metric.serviceId);
    const enriched = ann ? { ...metric, annotation: ann.annotation } : { ...metric, annotation: null };
    this.emitEnriched(enriched, offset);
  }
  processAnnotation(ann, offset, ts) {
    this.latestAnnotation.set(ann.serviceId, { annotation: ann.value, ts });
    // re-emit any buffered metrics for this service
    const buffered = this.metricBuffer.get(ann.serviceId) || [];
    for (const m of buffered) {
      this.emitEnriched({ ...m.metric, annotation: ann.value }, m.offset);
    }
    this.metricBuffer.set(ann.serviceId, []);
  }
  emitEnriched(record, offset) {
    // send to metrics_enriched and commit offset afterward
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes practical producer-side rate limiting, per-topic isolation, and basic backpressure, all at a beginner level but with real constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Per-topic state and ordering guarantees
- Token bucket rate limiting and refill logic
- Backpressure strategies: accept/queue vs reject with retry
- Minimal test plan to validate burst handling and latency

## Code Example
```javascript
class TokenBucket {
  constructor(capacity, rate) {
    this.capacity = capacity;
    this.rate = rate; // tokens per second
    this.tokens = capacity;
    this.last = Date.now();
  }
  tryRemove(n = 1) {
    const now = Date.now();
    const elapsed = (now - this.last) / 1000;
    this.tokens = Math.min(this.capacity, this.tokens + elapsed * this.rate);
    this.last = now;
    if (this.tokens >= n) { this.tokens -= n; return true; }
    return false;
  }
}
```

